<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <RootNamespace>OpenRamble.LinuxInput</RootNamespace>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      The Linux half of the two things the shell does to the rest of the desktop: watch the
      dictation key everywhere, and put the recognized text into somebody else's window. Its peers
      on the other platform are OpenRamble.App's KeyboardHook/HotkeyMonitor and TextInserter, which
      cannot be a library because they are Win32; this one can, because everything it touches is a
      device file or a subprocess. So it is one - and the whole wave is testable before the
      Avalonia shell that will consume it exists.

      The playbook puts these adapters in the future src/OpenRamble.App.Linux. This project is that
      code, extracted, and the shell will reference it rather than re-implement it.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="../OpenRamble.AppCore/OpenRamble.AppCore.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      The device seam and the subprocess seam are internal for the reason PipeWireCapture's
      recorder is: they are this assembly's vocabulary for talking to the kernel and to
      wl-clipboard, not a contract anyone else may build on. The tests drive both, which is the
      only way the permission failure, the hotplug rescan and the clipboard restore can be checked
      on a machine that is not the one the product runs on.
    -->
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="OpenRamble.LinuxInput.Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
